=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=
=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=
=0ABy Bill Murphy =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 Powered by Colony Spark [https://www.c=
olonyspark.com]=0A=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS=0ABRIEF=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AISSUE #22 =
=C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 AUGUST 28, 2026=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AHi ${first_=
name},=0A=0AAI adoption is not the day the tool goes live. It is the day yo=
ur people stop=0Agoing back to their own spreadsheet.=0A=0AEvery section in=
 this issue carries that theme. The Shift says nobody measures=0Awhether pe=
ople actually use what was deployed. From the Floor is an operations=0Adire=
ctor who built tools that worked and nobody used. The Stack is the one-page=
=0Aweekly record that catches it.=0A=0ABill=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE SHIFT=0A=
=0A=0AWe measure whether AI works. We do not measure whether anyone uses it=
.=0A=0AThe gap is not between companies that bought AI and companies that =
did not. It=0Ais between companies where people use the thing and companies=
 where the tool=0Asits there while the operation runs on the same spreadshe=
ets it always ran on.=0A=0AEvery AI conversation in manufacturing is still =
framed as a technology question.=0AWhich model, which vendor, which use cas=
e. The 2026 data says the technology=0Aquestion got settled and the behavio=
r question was never asked.=0A=0ASix signals from 2026=0A=0A=C2=B7 84% of m=
anufacturers agreed executive leadership is more enthusiastic about AI=0Ath=
an employees. In the same survey, 88% said they have the right in-house AI=
=0Aexpertise, but only 22% said their AI investment is concentrated on upsk=
illing=0Atheir own people (RSM US, July 2026=0A[https://rsmus.com/insights/=
industries/manufacturing/manufacturers-using-ai-2026.html]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 Ve=
ndor surveys put manufacturing AI adoption near 88%. The federal survey put=
s=0Aactual business AI use at 19.8% as of May 2026. Both are accurate. They=
 measure=0Adifferent things. One measures that a company has AI somewhere. =
The other=0Ameasures whether the business used it (U.S. Census Bureau, May =
2026=0A[https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2026/05/ai-use-businesses.ht=
ml]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 Usage splits hard by size. 37% at firms with 250+ employe=
es, 32% at 100 to=0A249, under 20% at the smallest firms.=0A=0A=C2=B7 The f=
ailure has a specific shape on the factory floor. Operators don't trust=0At=
he recommendations. Supervisors revert to spreadsheets. Engineers quietly=
=0Adisconnect the system to keep lines running. The problem is not the algo=
rithm.=0AIt is the absence of people in the process (Catalyst Connection, O=
ct 2025=0A[https://www.catalystconnection.org/news-blog/why-ai-can-often-fa=
il-on-the-factory-floor/]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 When AI pilots underperform, manufa=
cturers cite data quality (51%),=0Aintegration (43%), security or complianc=
e (34%), unclear ROI (30%), and adoption=0Aresistance (30%). Data quality a=
nd integration get budgets, owners, and=0Adashboards. Adoption gets a train=
ing email.=0A=0A=C2=B7 The measurement fix: Track engagement metrics, user =
confidence, decision=0Aconsistency, and collaboration alongside technical K=
PIs like accuracy or uptime.=0AThe best models mean little if no one uses t=
hem (Catalyst Connection, Oct 2025=0A[https://www.catalystconnection.org/ne=
ws-blog/why-ai-can-often-fail-on-the-factory-floor/]).=0A=0AAt our roundtab=
le on August 26, Robert Wyse=0A[https://www.linkedin.com/in/robert-wyse-3aa=
20884/], owner of Industronics=0AService Company [https://industronics.com/=
], described the signal he watches=0Afor: the point where employees get com=
fortable enough with a tool that they=0Astart advocating for it on their ow=
n, without being asked. It is an early=0Asignal, not a return. It also cost=
s nothing to watch, and almost nobody writes=0Ait down.=0A=0AThat is the pa=
ttern across all of it. The only proof of adoption is that the old=0Aworkar=
ound is gone, and nobody checks. Deployment gets a project plan. Uptime=0Ag=
ets a dashboard. Whether anyone asked for the thing gets nothing.=0A=0AFor =
every tool you deployed this year, can you name the workaround it was=0Asup=
posed to replace, and can you prove that workaround is gone.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=
=0AFROM THE FLOOR=0A=0A=0AWhy adoption stays invisible.=0A=0AMatt Gargas, D=
irector of US Operations, RDT, on tools that work and get ignored=0A=0AMatt=
 Gargas runs operations at RDT, a mid-market industrial manufacturer.=0ASpe=
aking with the Operations Brief Roundtable on August 26 about his early=0Ae=
xperience with AI deployment, he described a failure mode nobody talks abou=
t:=0Athe tools that work but nobody uses.=0A=0A"I can't tell you how many V=
BA spreadsheets I built that looked good and=0Acalculated quickly but never=
 got used. Everyone went back to their own=0Aspreadsheet."=0A=0AMatt Gargas=
=0A=0ADirector of US Operations, RDT =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 LinkedIn=0A[https:/=
/www.linkedin.com/in/mattgargas/]=0A=0AThe tools were not broken. They work=
ed. They looked good. They calculated fast.=0AThey were ignored. That is th=
e difference between technically finished and=0Aoperationally adopted, and =
it is the whole issue in two sentences. This failure=0Amode is not new eith=
er. It predates AI by twenty years. That is exactly why the=0AAI version wa=
s predictable.=0A=0AThe takeaway=0A=0AThe rollout is not the finish line. A=
 training completion report is not=0Aevidence. If the old spreadsheet is st=
ill open on somebody's second monitor=0Aninety days in, the deployment is a=
 line item, not a capability.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE STACK=0A=0A=0AThe Adopti=
on Ledger.=0A=0AA one-page weekly record that catches what dashboards miss.=
=0A=0ANot a dashboard. Dashboards are how the adoption question gets avoide=
d.=0A=0AAdoption has no owner in most operations. Data quality has an owner=
. Uptime has=0Aan owner. Whether Denise in scheduling still runs her own s=
preadsheet is=0Anobody's job. The Adoption Ledger is a one-page weekly reco=
rd per deployed tool,=0Aanswering one question: is this tool replacing the =
thing it was supposed to=0Areplace.=0A=0ASet up once, at deployment=0A=0A=
=C2=B7 Target task. One specific task this tool performs. If you cannot nam=
e one,=0Athat is already the finding.=0A=0A=C2=B7 The workaround it replace=
s. Name it exactly. "Manual process" is not an=0Aanswer. "Denise's shipping=
 spreadsheet on the shared drive" is an answer.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Expected users =
and how often. Daily, per shift, per order, per exception.=0A=0A=C2=B7 One =
operating metric. Cycle time, error rate, rework, or touches per order.=0AP=
ick it before go-live.=0A=0AUpdate weekly=0A=0A=C2=B7 Usage this week again=
st the expectation.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Workaround status: retired, shrinking, unch=
anged, or grown.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Exceptions with a reason code: too slow, did n=
ot trust it, could not access=0Ait, did not fit the process, faster my way,=
 nobody showed me, tool was wrong.=0A=0A=C2=B7 The operating metric reading=
.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Unprompted advocacy: did anyone ask for it or recommend it w=
ithout being=0Aasked.=0A=0AThe weekly decision=0A=0AOne line per tool=0A=0A=
Keep: usage meets expectation, workaround is shrinking or gone.=0A=0AChange=
: usage is short and the reason codes point at a fixable cause. Fix the=0An=
amed cause, set a review date.=0A=0AStop: four straight weeks of unchanged =
workaround status with no fixable reason.=0AWrite down why, so the next ven=
dor conversation starts from evidence.=0A=0AThree examples=0A=0AAI quoting =
assistant.=0A=0ATarget task: first-pass quote build. Workaround: the estima=
tor's personal=0Apricing spreadsheet. Expected on every quote under $50K. U=
sed on 9 of 22.=0AWorkaround unchanged. Top reason code: did not trust it o=
n nonstandard parts.=0ADecision: change. Pull nonstandard parts out of scop=
e and re-baseline in three=0Aweeks.=0A=0APredictive maintenance alerts on l=
ine 3.=0A=0AWorkaround: monthly walk-around plus operator intuition. All 11=
 alerts triaged=0Awithin a shift. Walk-around dropped to biweekly. Unplanne=
d downtime down. The=0Aline 2 supervisor asked to be added. Decision: keep =
and expand.=0A=0AAI meeting notes for production standup.=0A=0AWorkaround: =
the supervisor's notebook. Used 2 of 5 days. Workaround unchanged.=0AReason=
 code: faster my way. No operating metric was ever defined at deployment.=
=0ADecision: stop.=0A=0AThe reason codes are the whole build. "People are n=
ot using it" is not=0Aactionable. "Six exceptions this week, five coded too=
 slow" is a specification.=0ALog exceptions as they happen, thirty seconds =
each. If logging takes longer than=0Athe workaround, nobody logs it and the=
 ledger dies. Friday, fifteen minutes,=0Awalk the page per tool and make th=
e call. Monthly, look at reason codes across=0Aall tools. The same code sho=
wing up on four tools is a design finding, not a=0Apeople problem.=0A=0ATwo=
 ways to build it=0A=0APath 1: Internal build=0A=0AA shared sheet and a rec=
urring fifteen minutes on Friday. Start with the three=0Atools you spent th=
e most on this year. Run it four weeks before judging=0Aanything. The disci=
pline is the product, not the software.=0A=0APath 2: Through your partner=
=0A=0AAsk the ERP partner or integrator to instrument usage and report over=
ride rate,=0Aabandonment rate, and exception reasons alongside uptime. Most=
 can. Almost none=0Ado unless asked. Put it in the next statement of work.=
=0A=0AA tool nobody uses is not a tool. It is a line item.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=
=0ATHE OPERATIONS BRIEF=0A=0ABy Bill Murphy =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 Powered by C=
olony Spark [https://www.colonyspark.com]=0A=0AWatching people go back to t=
he spreadsheet, or already tracking adoption=0Ametrics=3F Hit reply, I read=
 every one. Bill
